\details{
\strong{Why \code{classifications}, not \code{prediction}:} SpeciesNet's ensemble
deliberately rolls a prediction up to genus/family/order/class/kingdom
whenever species-level confidence is insufficient (Markoff & Galaktionovs
2025, arXiv:2510.14594 -- precision over recall by design). The raw top-5
\code{classifications} block is the classifier's OWN candidate list, computed
before that rollup and before geofencing -- it still carries
species-level signal that the ensemble's own \code{prediction} field has
already discarded. This function treats \code{classifications} as the primary
multi-candidate source (mirroring \code{\link[=blast_sequences]{blast_sequences()}}/
\code{\link[=read_animl_output]{read_animl_output()}}'s multiple-hypotheses-per-query shape) precisely so
that species-level candidates reach TaxaLikely/TaxaAssign's own
prior-informed resolution instead of being silently pre-resolved by
SpeciesNet's conservative rollup. \code{ensemble_prediction}/
\code{ensemble_prediction_score} are retained only as reference metadata (e.g.
to compare against what TaxaID's own posterior later resolves to), not as
an alternative candidate source.

\strong{Filtering non-wildlife detections:} SpeciesNet assigns non-taxonomic
labels (\code{"animal"}, \code{"blank"}, \code{"human"}, \code{"vehicle"}) when no wildlife
taxon is identified, and a \code{"no cv result"} placeholder when the
classifier itself failed. All of these have \code{taxon_rank = NA}. Filter
before standardizing:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{match_df <- read_speciesnet_output("speciesnet_predictions.json") |>
  dplyr::filter(!is.na(taxon_rank))
